(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & ={(0.3)}^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.0189.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186490317 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<7) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{2}(0.25)+(0.75)^{3}(0.25)+(0.75)^{4}(0.25)+(0.75)^{5}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.3845.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186490319 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{5}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2436}{15625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186490323 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186490325 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.6)^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>4.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>4)=1-P(X\\le 4)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.6)^{0}(0.4)+(0.6)^{1}(0.4)+(0.6)^{2}(0.4)+(0.6)^{3}(0.4) \\\\  & =0.8704.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.8704 \\\\  & =0.1296.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186491130 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,x=\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}},\\,x=\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,x{y}^{2}=4,\\,x{y}^{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v=x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{3}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=u\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v},\\,\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186491144 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{({y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-5 \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 3 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,4).$\n\n![](images/fe/fe39efb538d3270028fa5892402d56a8ae0e061968ecca8c676d8ad9f482644e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,{u}^{2}-5\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186491164 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}+y \\\\ 2-y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}^{3}+y=u$ and $y=2-v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}+y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+2-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=2-v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,2-v=2\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,0).$\n\n![](images/60/60d8d3e30eba31683d1b6bb9bf399aa20f2bda37be1cb31bec431a4e27c0f54e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 0\\}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186491173 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}^{3}-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-y)^{4}-y=1\\,⟹\\,{u}^{4}-v=1\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{4}-1 \\\\ v=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ v=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt[4]{3},2).$\n\n![](images/5a/5a2e3fd6f3669f38834359f69e68b01a1e0d1438023418c5234d4071182f4808.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt[4]{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt[4]{3},\\,{u}^{4}-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186492015 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v-1=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c598c1c0d09fb26175cad17106bc4529165da619f35a5882ead0ba133d05c2c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}={(-2)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1-({u}^{2}-1))du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186492060 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(x-y)^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v+{u}^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{4} \\\\ v=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,2).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/81/81ff220903cbe53cebf5e8f8b704e2a316be6092e1f14e527a7321e8f61bffb8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}((3-{u}^{4})-2)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4}))\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186492840 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-4y=-1 \\\\ y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=2$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=7.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."] [#edb/ref 17592186492874 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k-5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k-5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=3 \\\\ (k-7)y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493908 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5\\cdot 6+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-28.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+(-28)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493914 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+2y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493923 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=∓\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $-\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its   maximum value is $\\sqrt{5}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493936 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ 2+(-1)+z & =0 \\\\ 1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ -2+1+z & =0 \\\\ -1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,-1,-1),\\,(-2,1,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,-1,-1)$ | $(-2,1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $5$ | $-5$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493939 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,\\,2y,\\,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493941 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=2x-y+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493964 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-4y-2z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-4,-2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 4=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-2\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{2}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(1)-4y-2(-1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(-1)-4y-2(1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1),\\,(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1)$ | $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $7$ | $-9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493972 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,2,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $x=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,1)\\,\\text{or}\\,(x,y)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ 1-1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ -1-(-1)-z & =2 \\\\ -1+1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,-2),\\,(-1,-1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,-2)$ | $(-1,-1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,1,-2),$ and its maximum value is $8.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186495152 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{bmatrix} & =(3k-2)\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -30+35k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6(3k-2) \\\\ (5k)(3k-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -30+35k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18k+12 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-10k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=-18k+12 \\\\ -30+35k=15{k}^{2}-10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-6=-18k+12\\,⟹\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30+35k & =15{k}^{2}-10k \\\\ -30+35\\cdot 1 & =15\\cdot {1}^{2}-10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5 & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496233 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-1$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z=-2 \\\\ (\\,3u-5z=-17\\,)\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z=-2 \\\\ -3u+5z=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $u,$ and solve the resulting equation for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3u}+z & =-2 \\\\ -\\cancel{3u}+5z & =17 \\\\ (z+5z) & =(-2+17) \\\\ 6z & =15 \\\\ z & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $z=\\frac{5}{2}.$ However, we still need to find $u.$ To do this, we substitute $z=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u+z & =-2 \\\\ 3u+\\frac{5}{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3u & =-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,z=\\frac{5}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496252 "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}21x-9y=0 & \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5y-15x+10=0 & \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can isolate $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-15x+10 & =0 \\\\ 5y & =15x-10 \\\\ y & =3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $3x-2$  into equation $(1)$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21x-9y & =0 \\\\ 21x-9(3x-2) & =0 \\\\ 21x-27x+18 & =0 \\\\ -6x+18 & =0 \\\\ 18 & =6x \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=3$ into $y=3x-2,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-2 \\\\ y & =3(3)-2 \\\\ y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3,\\,y=7.$ So,\n\n$$\ny-x=7-3=4.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496256 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3x-2y=22\\,)\\,\\times (4) \\\\ 5x+8y=14\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12x-8y=88 \\\\ 5x+8y=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-\\cancel{8y} & =88 \\\\ 5x+\\cancel{8y} & =14 \\\\ (12x+5x) & =(88+14) \\\\ 17x & =102\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=6.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=6$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =22 \\\\ 3(6)-2y & =22 \\\\ 18-2y & =22 \\\\ -2y & =4 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=6,\\,y=-2,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,6,-2\\,).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496264 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496289 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4r+9v=3\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 8r-2v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-8r-18v=-6 \\\\ 8r-2v=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{8r}-18v & =-6 \\\\ \\cancel{8r}-2v & =1 \\\\ (-2v-18v) & =(1-6) \\\\ -20v & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-20v.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496295 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=250.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=250\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ y & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-3y=∓15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-15,5),\\,(15,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-15,5)$ | $(15,-5)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $50$ | $-50$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-15,5),$ and its maximum value is $50.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496297 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+12}{3}=\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{4}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t-12 \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=4t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 3(3t-12)^{2}+2(4t)^{2} & =2(4t)^{2} \\\\ 3(3t-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3t-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3t-12 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =12 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3(4)-12=0 \\\\ y=4(4)=16 \\\\ z=4(4)=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,16,16).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496304 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-5$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4u+5p=24 \\\\ (\\,5u+p=9\\,)\\,\\times (-5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4u+5p=24 \\\\ -25u-5p=-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $p,$ and solve the resulting equation for $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u+\\cancel{5p} & =24 \\\\ -25u-\\cancel{5p} & =-45 \\\\ (4u-25u) & =(24-45) \\\\ -21u & =-21 \\\\ u & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u=1.$ However, we still need to find $p.$ To do this, we substitute $u=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5u+p & =9 \\\\ 5(1)+p & =9 \\\\ 5+p & =9 \\\\ p & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=1,\\,p=4,$ which can be expressed as $(1,4).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496305 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $7$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+7y=-20 \\\\ (\\,4x-y=-7\\,)\\,\\times (7)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+7y=-20 \\\\ 28x-7y=-49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+\\cancel{7y} & =-20 \\\\ 28x-\\cancel{7y} & =-49 \\\\ (-5x+28x) & =(-20-49) \\\\ 23x & =-69\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-3.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=-3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-y & =-7 \\\\ 4(-3)-y & =-7 \\\\ -12-y & =-7 \\\\ -y & =5 \\\\ y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=-5,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,-3,-5\\,).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496314 "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p-6q=-18 & \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 4p+3q+2=0 & \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(1),$ we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-6q & =-18 \\\\ 3p & =6q-18 \\\\ p & =2q-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=2q-6$ into equation $(2)$ and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2q-6)+3q+2 & =0 \\\\ 8q-24+3q+2 & =0 \\\\ 11q & =22 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $q=2$ into equation $(1),$ then we can find the value of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-6(2) & =-18 \\\\ 3p-12 & =-18 \\\\ 3p & =-6 \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-2,\\,q=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,2).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496322 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-2x+y=6\\,)\\,\\times (-4) \\\\ 3x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-4y=-24 \\\\ 3x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-\\cancel{4y} & =-24 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ (8x+3x) & =(-24+2) \\\\ 11x & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=-2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(-2)+y & =6 \\\\ 4+y & =6 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2,\\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,2).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186496330 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-1$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,x-5y=-12\\,)\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ x+3y=20\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+5y=12 \\\\ x+3y=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{x}+5y & =12 \\\\ \\cancel{x}+3y & =20 \\\\ (5y+3y) & =(12+20) \\\\ 8y & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186497345 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186497359 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 4x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)(1-0.25(1))-3(1)y & =0 \\\\ 3-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186497380 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{7}{6}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -13 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{13}{6} & \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{2}{x}_{2}=-13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}{x}_{2}=\\frac{13}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second, third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 2 & =4 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186498520 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=7t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(4t)={e}^{4t} \\\\ r(7t)={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186498556 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $200\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=200\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=200I=200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{1}{25}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{425},\\beta =\\frac{1}{425}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}-\\frac{4}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-4,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-4+1=-3.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499467 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{9}{2}-6I-8q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{2}-6I(0)-8q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-6\\cdot 1-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{7}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}=-4A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499470 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(11) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=11-5I-4q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =11-5I(0)-4q(0) \\\\  & =11-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =-4A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}=-4A-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{17}{6}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499479 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{3} \\\\ v={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499482 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(25) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-6t}$ and $I={e}^{-3t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=25-9I-18q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =25-9I(0)-18q(0) \\\\  & =25-9\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-18\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =A{e}^{-6(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-6A{e}^{-6t}-3B{e}^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{43}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{43}{4} & =-6A{e}^{-6(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} & =-6A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{43}{4}=-6A-3B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{29}{6}$ and $B=\\frac{73}{12}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499485 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{21}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ 2=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499494 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-6\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},$ $\\beta =\\frac{2}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+2=-1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499539 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{36}{145})}^{2}+{(\\frac{32}{145})}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [#edb/ref 17592186499542 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 60\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+3(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-7\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-9\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-7\\alpha +9\\beta =30\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -9\\alpha -7\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{27}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-21$ and $Q=27,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-21+27=6.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186499558 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta =\\frac{16}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{72}{125})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{125})}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [#edb/ref 17592186500304 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $3\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}2(-t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={8t-{t}^{2}|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+8x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186500307 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,{e}^{x+y}=2,\\,{e}^{x+y}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,{e}^{x+y}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv={e}^{uy+y}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡v=uy+y\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186500341 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186500364 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+3y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+3y)}^{2}+y=5\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=5\\,⟹\\,v=5-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=5-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,4).$\n\n![](images/94/9427985b45271bec39577b35334311c11b71983d1cc5f8b9ac35c6fe88f29d98.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,4\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186500373 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $2x-3y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-3y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0).$\n\n![](images/17/172daaae3de5212c5e182965b66bf2afcade59e2d5eb156c7dbff214e3bfb040.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186501183 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $5\\le x\\le 10,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<5 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1, & x>10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{5}^{x}\\frac{2}{75}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{75}{t}^{2}|}_{5}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75},\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186501225 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186502090 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $q$ and $t$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{q,t\\}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186502110 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{3,5\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed92f46f57aefc4d9c7445f044f1b35adb89950c16f331aff35c02445b607c95.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $3$ and $5$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186503676 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16+{t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(16+{t}^{3})\\,j & =c(i+3{t}^{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 16+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 16 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =2\\,i+(16+{2}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+24\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(2,24).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186503679 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-2)\\,i+\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j & =c((2t-2)i+\\,j) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j & =c(2t-2)i+c\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t+2=c(2t-2) \\\\ t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $t-1=c.$ Substituting $c=t-1$ into the first equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t+2 & =(t-1)(2t-2) \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+2 & =2{t}^{2}-4t+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =({0}^{2}-2(0)+2)\\,i+(0-1)\\,j=2\\,i-\\,j, \\\\ r(2) & =({2}^{2}-2(2)+2)\\,i+(2-1)\\,j=2\\,i+\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186503699 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2t=0 \\\\ 4t=c \\\\ 2+2t=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=4.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2-{1}^{2}\\,,\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\,,\\,2+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2,3).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186503739 "The point $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{t}=2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)}}\\,j+\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the curve at the point $P(1,2,2)$ is $\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186503747 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,-1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =⟨c,-c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=c \\\\ 6t+4=-c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2{t}^{2}.$ Substituting $c=2{t}^{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+4 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+6t+4 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t+2 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=-2$ and $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3},3(-1)^{2}+4(-1)⟩=⟨-\\frac{2}{3},-1⟩, \\\\ r(-2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-2)^{3},3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)⟩=⟨-\\frac{16}{3},4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)$ and $(-\\frac{16}{3},4).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186504627 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-3x+2y-5)=y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-3x+2y-5)=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨y-3,\\,x+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y-3=0 \\\\ x+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=-2,$ while the first equation gives $y=3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-2)(3)-3(-2)+2(3)-5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(-2,3,1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186504635 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186504653 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186504691 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation we get $y=1,$ while the second equation gives $x=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=0\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,1,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186504709 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186506531 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,3],$ we have a line with a slope of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x$ that ends at $(3,1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(3,\\infty ),$ we have a horizontal line that passes through the point $(3,2).$ So the graph is the line $y=2$ that starts at $(3,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186506574 "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have $f(x)=0.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=1.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186506586 "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,-2),$ we have $f(x)=3.$ Notice that the point $x=-2$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,1),$ we have $f(x)=-2.$ Notice that the point $x=-2$ is included and the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=2.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\,,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & -2\\le x<1 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507402 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{19}x\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{19}x{[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}]}_{y=4}^{y=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{57}x{[{y}^{3/2}]}_{y=4}^{y=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{57}x({9}^{3/2}-{4}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1.9) & ={\\int }_{1.9}^{\\infty }{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1.9}^{2}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{1.9}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{2}-{1.9}^{2}] \\\\  & =0.13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507417 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{x}({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0.5}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =[{0.5}^{3/2}]-[{0}^{3/2}] \\\\  & ≈0.3536.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507428 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$\n\n![](images/f6/f65ef4fc9d7ae895bc7ba8fe99bc7b03d8b26c92611fcbc43f600aa2e0c30301.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}})}^{2}\\cdot y-{0}^{2}\\cdot y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-{y}^{2})y-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4y-{y}^{3}] \\\\  & =y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507441 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3={X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-2=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-1){X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s)=3 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)+(s-4){X}_{2}(s)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=2-(s-4){X}_{2}(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1)[2-(s-4){X}_{2}(s)]-2{X}_{2}(s) & =3 \\\\ 2(s-1)-[(s-1)(s-4)+2]{X}_{2}(s) & =3 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-5s+6){X}_{2}(s) & =3-2(s-1) \\\\ -(s-2)(s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =-2s+5 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2s-5}{(s-2)(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =2-(s-4){X}_{2}(s) \\\\  & =2-(s-4)\\cdot \\frac{2s-5}{(s-2)(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(s-2)(s-3)-(s-4)(2s-5)}{(s-2)(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s-8}{(s-2)(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3s-8}{(s-2)(s-3)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{2s-5}{(s-2)(s-3)}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507447 "At $x=-2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}g(x) & =g(-2) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(a{x}^{2}+b) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{2}+b \\\\  & =4a+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}g(x) \\\\ 2 & =4a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+b) \\\\  & =a(0)^{2}+b \\\\  & =0+b \\\\  & =b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=2 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can replace $b$ by $6$ in the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+b & =2 \\\\ 4a+6 & =2 \\\\ 4a & =-4 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $x=0$ for $a=-1$ and $b=6.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507448 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=0 \\\\ 6\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{54\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507462 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=4{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-3=4(-\\frac{1}{s+1})+2{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507471 "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(-1)+3b \\\\  & =-2a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -5 & =-2a+3b \\\\ 2a-3b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(2)+3b \\\\  & =4a+3b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4a+3b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-3b=5 \\\\ 4a+3b=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can replace $a$ by $\\frac{3}{2}$ into the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{3}{2})-3b & =5 \\\\ 3-3b & =5 \\\\ -3b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507488 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/df/dfa15482cfa0e8fdc9e98b05a71d2b64a89b3886da44f6966fee9f1b5e096eef.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}15{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =15{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{15({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507508 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186507519 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2(\\frac{4}{s-3})+{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-1)(s-3)}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186508076 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nz-3=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-(-5))^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(2,-5,3).$\n Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186508214 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}b=135-11a \\\\ 7b+162a=435\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=135-11a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7b+162a & =435 \\\\ 7(135-11a)+162a & =435 \\\\ 945-77a+162a & =435 \\\\ 945+85a & =435 \\\\ 85a & =-510 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-6$ into $b=135-11a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =135-11(-6) \\\\ b & =201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-6,\\,b=201.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186508913 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $-7.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $7,$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $7,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-7$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+y=8\\,)\\,\\times (7) \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-35x+7y=56 \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35x+\\cancel{7y} & =56 \\\\ 8x-\\cancel{7y} & =25 \\\\ (-35x+8x) & =(56+25) \\\\ -27x & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $7,$ and add the equations*."] [#edb/ref 17592186508917 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $10,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-10,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ (\\,2x+5y=-4\\,)\\,\\times (-5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ -10x-25y=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10x}-3y & =36 \\\\ -\\cancel{10x}-25y & =20 \\\\ (-3y-25y) & =(36+20) \\\\ -28y & =56 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-5,$ and add the equations*."] [#edb/ref 17592186508926 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the  first  equation is $6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ (\\,2x+6y=12\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ -6x-18y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{6x}+13y & =21 \\\\ -\\cancel{6x}-18y & =-36 \\\\ (13y-18y) & =(21-36) \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+13y & =21 \\\\ 6x+13(3) & =21 \\\\ 6x+39 & =21 \\\\ 6x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,3).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186508927 "Notice that the $q$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $q$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3p+2q=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p-4q=-22 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-6p-\\cancel{4q} & =-22 \\\\ 5p+\\cancel{4q} & =19 \\\\ (-6p+5p) & =(-22+19) \\\\ -p & =-3 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $p=3.$ However, we still need to find $q.$ To do this, we substitute $p=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+2q & =11 \\\\ 3(3)+2q & =11 \\\\ 9+2q & =11 \\\\ 2q & =2 \\\\ q & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186508932 "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ (\\,p+q=4\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ -2p-2q=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3p+\\cancel{2q} & =11 \\\\ -2p-\\cancel{2q} & =-8 \\\\ (3p-2p) & =(11-8) \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $q,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-2,$ and add the equations*."] [#edb/ref 17592186510364 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4(5) & =-11 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186510376 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+((t-1)+4)^{2}+((2t+2)-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+(t+3)^{2}+(2t)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+6t+9)+4{t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6t+9 & =9 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6t & =0 \\\\ t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=(0)-1=-1 \\\\ z=2(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-1,2).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=(-1)-1=-2 \\\\ z=2(-1)+2=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-1,2)$ and $(-1,-2,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186510412 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{2}=y=z+2=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t-1 \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =4{z}^{2} \\\\ (2t-1)^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =4(t-2)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t+1+3{t}^{2} & =4({t}^{2}-4t+4) \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t+1+3{t}^{2} & =4{t}^{2}-16t+16 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+12t-15 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}+4t-5) & =0 \\\\ 3(t+5)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-5,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-5)-1=-11 \\\\ y=-5 \\\\ z=-5-2=-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-11,-5,-7).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)-1=1 \\\\ y=1 \\\\ z=1-2=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-11,-5,-7)$ and $(1,1,-1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186510447 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 3\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -10\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-10.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}(-10) & =-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}{x}_{1} & =10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186511466 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186511479 "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 2a-4-2b+10 & =0 \\\\ 2a-2b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+b\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ a+3+b-6 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-2b=-6 \\\\ a+b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-4b=-12\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-2(3) & =-6 \\\\ 2a-6 & =-6 \\\\ 2a & =0 \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=0-3=-3.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186511482 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ -2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] [#edb/ref 17592186511496 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186511507 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =-3[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [#edb/ref 17592186511526 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ 2xy+8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] [#edb/ref 17592186511549 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186511564 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{17}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{34}}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512878 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-14{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512883 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+4z=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,4⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=4\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\mu x=0 \\\\ \\lambda =1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the first equation above, we must have $x=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(1) & =4 \\\\ y+4 & =4 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(-1) & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =4 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0,1),\\,(0,8,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,8,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,8,-1),$ and its  maximum value is $10.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512889 "Since $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(1) \\\\  & =a(1)^{3}-2(1)^{2}+b(1)+1 \\\\  & =a-2+b+1 \\\\  & =a+b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $4$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =f(-2) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+b(-2)+1 \\\\  & =-8a-8-2b+1 \\\\  & =-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=a+b-1 \\\\ 4=-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+b=1 \\\\ 8a+2b=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b,$ we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ and we add the resulting equation to the second equation. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2a-2b=-2 \\\\ +\\,\\,\\, & \\,8a+2b=-11 \\\\  & \\,6a=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $a=-\\frac{13}{6}.$ Finally, we substitute it into $a+b=1$ to find $b=\\frac{19}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $a-b=-\\frac{16}{3}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512900 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512908 "Notice that the $a$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5,$ then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10,$ and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,5a+4b=7\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ (\\,-2a+6b=20\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+8b=14 \\\\ -10a+30b=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10a}+8b & =14 \\\\ -\\cancel{10a}+30b & =100 \\\\ (8b+30b) & =(14+100) \\\\ 38b & =114 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+4b & =7 \\\\ 5a+4(3) & =7 \\\\ 5a+12 & =7 \\\\ 5a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-1$ and $b=3,$ which can be written as $(-1,3).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512930 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512931 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3xz+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3z,\\,2y,\\,3x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186512949 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=2{X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-2){X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)+(s-3){X}_{2}(s)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)[3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)]+2{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 3(s-2)-[(s-2)(s-3)-2]{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-5s+4){X}_{2}(s) & =4-3(s-2) \\\\ -(s-1)(s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =-3s+10 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s) \\\\  & =3-(s-3)\\cdot \\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-1)(s-4)-(s-3)(3s-10)}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s-18}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186514483 "We have the piecewise function\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ 8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =p(3) \\\\  & =k+10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(3)-\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3} \\\\  & =24-18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $p(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) \\\\ k+10 & =6 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ when $k=-4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186515519 "The given points are ${{(-5,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(1,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,4) & =(\\frac{-5+a}{2},\\frac{3+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\frac{-5+a}{2} \\\\ 4=\\frac{3+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}a=7 \\\\ b=5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(7,5)$ and $a+b=12.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186515576 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-4\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-4L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3=2{X}_{1}(s)-\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-0={X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3 & =2{X}_{1}(s)-\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s-2){X}_{1}(s) & =3-\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s-2){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{3s-4}{s} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{3s-4}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s) & ={X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{2}(s) & ={X}_{1}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s-4}{s(s-2)} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s-4}{s(s-1)(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system  in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3s-4}{s(s-2)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-4}{s(s-1)(s-2)}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186515586 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =2(\\frac{1}{2}){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186515593 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)-3\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-3L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2=4{X}_{1}(s)-\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2 & =4{X}_{1}(s)-\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =2-\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ (s-4){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2s-3}{s} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{2s-3}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)+1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-2){X}_{2}(s) & =-{X}_{1}(s)-1 \\\\ (s-2){X}_{2}(s) & =-(\\frac{2s-3}{s(s-4)})-1 \\\\ (s-2){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-(2s-3)-s(s-4)}{s(s-4)} \\\\ (s-2){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-2s+3-{s}^{2}+4s}{s(s-4)} \\\\ (s-2){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+2s+3}{s(s-4)} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+2s+3}{s(s-2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2s-3}{s(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-{s}^{2}+2s+3}{s(s-2)(s-4)}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186515597 "The given points are ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(0,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(-3,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (-3,3) & =(\\frac{a+0}{2},\\frac{b+1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3=\\frac{a}{2} \\\\ 3=\\frac{b+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}a=-6 \\\\ b=5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=-6$ and $b=5.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186516797 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-4y+z+16 & =0 \\\\ 6(4-t)-4(2+3t)+(-6+5t) & =-16 \\\\ 24-6t-8-12t-6+5t & =-16 \\\\ -13t & =-26 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=2$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4-2=2 \\\\ y=2+3(2)=8 \\\\ z=-6+5(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(2,8,4).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186516801 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y-8z & =186 \\\\ 2(8+3t)+5(-6+7t)-8(2t) & =186 \\\\ 16+6t-30+35t-16t & =186 \\\\ 25t & =200 \\\\ t & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=8$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=8+3(8)=32 \\\\ y=-6+7(8)=50 \\\\ z=2(8)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(32,50,16).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186516816 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[1,5],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X\\le 5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186516836 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3},$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{4}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518836 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-4) & =-4(2) \\\\ & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,-4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-8)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-4,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518840 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((-2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(9) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518842 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,-2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,-2) & =2(3) \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-2)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(3) & =(6)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =6 x - 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,-2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=6 x - 15\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518849 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,1,-10)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & =3(2) \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,1)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-10) & =(6)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =6 x - 22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,-10)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=6 x - 22\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518860 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,- \\pi,-11)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((3)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(-1.0)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =-1.0 + 1.0 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,- \\pi)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(-11) & =(-1 + \\pi)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =- x + \\pi x - 3 \\pi - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,- \\pi,-11)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- x + \\pi x - 3 \\pi - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518867 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2 \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,2 \\pi,12)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,2 \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,2 \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2 \\pi) & =(2 \\pi)\\cos((3)(2 \\pi))+\\cos(2 \\pi) \\\\ & =(2 \\pi)(1)+(1) \\\\ & =1 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,2 \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,2 \\pi)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(12) & =(1 + 2 \\pi)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =x + 2 \\pi x - 6 \\pi + 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,2 \\pi,12)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\pi \\\\ z=x + 2 \\pi x - 6 \\pi + 9\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518868 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,1,11)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,1) & =-4(3) \\\\ & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,1)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(11) & =(-12)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =47 - 12 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,1,11)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=47 - 12 x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518874 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =4(-2) \\\\ & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,2)}(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(8) & =(-8)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z & =- 8 x - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=- 8 x - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518878 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,- \\pi,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1.0)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- 1.0 \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- \\pi)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(1) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x + 3 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,- \\pi,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\pi x - x + 3 + 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186518891 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- 3 \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,- 3 \\pi,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- 3 \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,- 3 \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,- 3 \\pi) & =(- 3 \\pi)\\cos((2)(- 3 \\pi))+\\cos(- 3 \\pi) \\\\ & =(- 3 \\pi)(1.0)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- 3.0 \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,- 3 \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- 3 \\pi)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(5) & =(- 3 \\pi - 1)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =- 3 \\pi x - x + 7 + 6 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,- 3 \\pi,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- 3 \\pi \\\\ z=- 3 \\pi x - x + 7 + 6 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519513 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [4x+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x+y}{4})}{(\\frac{2x+1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+y}{4x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519525 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2})}{(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2})} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519535 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{64}\\cdot (y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}, & \\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32})}{(\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}, & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519536 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3y}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x{e}^{-3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6x{e}^{-3y}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519545 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{y}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{36}+\\frac{xy}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{36}+\\frac{3y}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+3}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12})}{(\\frac{2y+3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519548 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519563 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$  \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<y<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{35}{36})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519578 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5y\\sqrt{x}}{(\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519584 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x})}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519592 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519605 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{14}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{14}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{14}\\cdot [27-(-1)] \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14})}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{28}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186519607 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{8})}{(\\frac{3y+1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{3y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{3y+1}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521461 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $7\\in \\{2,4,6,10\\}.$ Since $7$ is not an element of the set $\\{2,4,6,10\\},$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[7\\in \\{2,4,6,10\\}]=0.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521476 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $0\\le 3.$ Since $0$ is less than or equal to $3,$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[0\\le 3]=1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521478 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}.$ Since $9$ is not an element of the set $\\{1,2,3,5\\},$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}]=0.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521491 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{B}(x)$ of a set $B$ equals $1$ if $x\\in B$ and $0$ if $x\\notin B.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(x)=1[x\\in B].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(4)=1[4\\in B]=1[4\\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}].\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521492 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{E}(x)$ of a set $E$ equals $1$ if $x\\in E$ and $0$ if $x\\notin E.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(x)=1[x\\in E].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(9)=1[9\\in E]=1[9\\in \\{1,4,9\\}].\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521512 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}.$ Since $3$ is an element of the set $\\{1,3,6,8\\},$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}]=1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521515 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2$ equals ${x}_{i}-1$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $2$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2\n$$\n\nadds ${x}_{i}-1$ for nonnegative values and $2$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-3\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[1\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[6\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[-2\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2 & =2+(1-1)+(6-1)+2+(0-1) \\\\  & =2+0+5+2-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521540 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9<3.$ Since $9$ is not less than $3,$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9<3]=0.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521549 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 2]=1[-3\\ge 2]=0$ (since $-3\\ge 2$ is false)\n\n- $1[-1\\le x<2]=1[-1\\le -3<2]=0$ (since $-1\\le -3$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =1[-3\\ge 2]\\cdot (-5)^{2}+1[-1\\le -3<2]\\cdot 1+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ only the third term contributes to the result."] [#edb/ref 17592186521550 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[-3>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[6>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 6+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0+6+0+2+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521551 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[4\\in C]$ has $x=4$ and $A=C.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[4\\in C]={1}_{C}(4).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521554 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+4xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+4x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)(1-0.5(1))-2(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,0.75).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521559 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge -1]=1[-3\\ge -1]=0$ (since $-3\\ge -1$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =1[-3\\ge -1]\\cdot (-3)^{2}+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result."] [#edb/ref 17592186521560 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.05x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(20,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.05(2))-3(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-6y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,0.6).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(20,0),\\,(2,0.6).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521564 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[8\\in F]$ has $x=8$ and $A=F.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[8\\in F]={1}_{F}(8).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521566 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[10\\in E]$ has $x=10$ and $A=E.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[10\\in E]={1}_{E}(10).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186521570 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-7>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[8>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-2>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot 8+1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0+0+8+9+0 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186522724 "Let $X$ represent the maximum daily temperature (rounded to the nearest degree Celsius). From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{-15,-14,…,2\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\ge 0).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-15,-14,…,2\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-15,-14,…,2\\}$ has $|S|=2-(-15)+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=-15,-14,…,2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\ge 0$ are $0,1,2.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 0)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186522787 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{12\\lambda }+2B{e}^{12\\lambda }+6(6A\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }+B{e}^{12\\lambda }+12B\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+6(6A\\lambda +B+12B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+36A\\lambda +6B+72B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+8(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1+6\\lambda  \\\\ 1+36\\lambda  & 8+72\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (8+72\\lambda )-(1+6\\lambda )(1+36\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 48\\lambda +432{\\lambda }^{2}-(1+42\\lambda +216{\\lambda }^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 216{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda +1)(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}B=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=B,$ where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =B{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}A+\\frac{4}{3}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-4B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-4B{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/3}(x-4) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/3}(x-4).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186523996 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$"]}
